+Projection and MIP Techniques are image-order rendering methods that generate 2D images from 3D volumetric data by aggregating scalar values along viewing rays, rather than simulating light transport via opacity and color blending. Unlike standard alpha compositing which preserves depth cues through accumulation, these methods are typically order-independent and discard intermediate sample information to highlight specific intensity ranges (e.g., maximum or minimum values) or total integrated density [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial][S4]. They are primarily used in medical imaging to visualize high-contrast structures like bones or vessels, or to approximate physical projections like X-rays [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial][S4].
 
